WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.230
MARCEL TANNER: My name is Marcel Tanner.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:19.460
I'm from the Swiss Tropical and Public Health institute in Basel, Switzerland,

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.070
and I'm also Professor of Epidemiology, Medical Parasitology,

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.650
and Public Health at the University of Basel,

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:32.330
as well as President of the Swiss Academy of Sciences.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:38.510
Today we would like to talk a little bit about how we reach elimination

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:43.550
of malaria by looking on what information we have to collect

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:48.800
and how we have to use this information swiftly in order to cut transmission,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.340
means eliminate malaria in the different parts of the world.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:59.750
Therefore, our learning objectives are to start with how we are monitoring

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.840
and evaluate our malaria control and elimination programs,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.440
but how we also do, more specifically, out of this classic remedy

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.390
malaria surveillance.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:17.510
We would like to explain a little bit on the different strategies of malaria monitoring

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:22.820
and evaluation and how we reach out of these concepts really

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.680
to surveillance-response approaches.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.430
And we then understand how the surveillance-response approaches are

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:33.170
not only theoretically propagated, but really put

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:39.310
into a specific endemic setting in order to serve

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:42.320
our programs of malaria elimination.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.020
And how, at the end, these parts of surveillance and response

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:55.280
are part of the overall public health response in our malaria elimination

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.100
and, finally, eradication programs.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.330
We will have the strategies for surveillance-elimination

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:06.230
in different settings that we look at, and then we

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.930
have an in-depth look on the data collection

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:13.310
and on how we make decisions out of this data that we collect,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:18.200
and how we actually move from collecting data, monitoring and evaluation,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.120
to it's surveillance-responsive approaches.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.200
As well as we will look at specific case studies

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.410
that have used this approach successfully

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.080
to reach malaria elimination.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.080
And of course, the final aim is to understand

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.210
that with these surveillance-response approaches,

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:39.170
we not only achieve elimination, but also

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.010
we really tackle the problem when it is worse

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:49.800
and where we actually look for the best equitable approaches in reaching

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.040
elimination.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.940
The first part will be really to look at strategies for surveillance systems

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.330
in different elimination settings.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:03.520
The basis of this is that we have in our population

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.210
in any malaria endemic area three types of populations.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.390
We have the not infected asymptomatic people.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:15.050
We have the infected asymptomatic people.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:18.740
And we have infected symptomatic people.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:24.350
And for each of the three population groups we have to do the same thing.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:28.790
We have actually to see that the not infected asymptomatics do not

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.440
get infected.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:35.090
We have to see that the infected asymptomatic cannot continue

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.450
to transmit.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:41.330
So that means we treat them so that the reservoir of the infection

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.160
is eliminated.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.010
And we have the infected symptomatic people,

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.820
where the goal is that, first of all, they are not getting sick

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.880
or if they are sick they actually are cured,

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.460
and we also prevent that they further transmit to the mosquitoes.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:03.330
And if we do this to all the three population groups,

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.969
then we can achieve the interruption of transmission.

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:07.760
And that's the aim.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.880
Elimination means interruption of transmission.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.279
It does not just mean reducing of morbidity.

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:21.260
And in order to reach eradication, you must actually

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.730
bring three building blocks together.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:27.530
First of all, you have to detect all the people who

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.720
have parasites-- the reservoir.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.760
Secondly, you have to cure the people, so you have to eliminate the reservoir.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.630
And of course, you have to prevent that any parasites are taken up

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.250
by the mosquitoes.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.310
And it's only if the three building blocks

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:48.650
are put together that we can achieve elimination and eradication.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:54.290
The key issue on this whole thing is that it occurs concurrently.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:59.070
You cannot do a little bit of detection, a little bit of cure,

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:01.430
and a little bit of prevention of transmission.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:06.680
You must do these three things together in a given endemic area--

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.340
in a given transmission pocket.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.810
Surveillance is key in order to detect the parasite.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:16.310
Surveillance is key in order to detect people who carry the parasites

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:19.700
or could transmit malaria.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:24.760
And here is a graph from the malaria report of 2015 already.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:30.110
And it said actually that surveillance systems are unfortunately weakest

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.470
where the highest burden is there.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:35.540
And we just take this as a reminder to say

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:40.550
that where surveillance is not functioning,

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:44.510
our control and elimination efforts are not functioning.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.680
That's the take-home message.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:52.620
And after having not just published this information,

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:59.160
WHO has published a new version of the surveillance manual.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.930
The surveillance manual is not just a book with recipes.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:09.170
It just emphasizes how important it is to really detect parasites,

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:16.730
to understand what a transmission pocket is, in order to really act,

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:22.310
as we said, simultaneously on asymptomatic and symptomatic people

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.450
in order to cut transmission.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:28.760
This has been launched in 2012, and unfortunately,

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:34.070
we must say today this message of having rigorous surveillance

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:37.970
and having followed it by an adequate public health response

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:43.450
is still not translated into action in many, many places.

